  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The most budget-conscious and effective solution is to use a <b>Lambda function that periodically checks your Auto Scaling group and updates your Route 53 DNS record when a new instance is started</b>. This approach requires minimal resources, incurs only small compute costs (you only pay for Lambda executions), and does not rely on more expensive infrastructure.<br><br>Let’s examine the other options:</p><ul><li><i>CloudWatch Event rule triggers Lambda for CNAME updates</i>: While possible, this method assumes you can set a CNAME to an EC2 instance, which is not feasible for root domains and may not be reliable when the instance is replaced. Plus, it introduces complexity and may have additional overhead versus the straightforward polling approach.</li><li><i>Alias record in Route 53 pointing to ASG ARN</i>: Route 53 does not support alias records or direct bindings to Auto Scaling group ARNs. You must reference resources like load balancers or CloudFront distributions, making this option invalid.</li><li><i>Network load balancer in front of the Auto Scaling group</i>: This method requires launching a load balancer, which is unnecessary with a single instance. It adds extra cost and architectural overhead for your small use case.</li></ul><p><b>The Lambda automation approach is cost-effective, simple, and directly suited for a minimal, personal project with a single changing instance.</b> As a tip, trigger the Lambda via Amazon CloudWatch Events (EventBridge) on EC2 state changes or run it on a schedule with a minimal time interval. This ensures Route 53 is always up to date with your current instance details at the lowest ongoing cost.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are managing a small, personal project. Your application is running inside an Auto Scaling group, always with just one Amazon EC2 instance. Sometimes, that instance gets terminated and a replacement is started—each time, the instance’s public DNS name and IP address change. You own a Route 53 hosted zone and want to direct user traffic to this instance by creating a record set. You need a minimal-cost and automated solution to keep the record updated as instances change. <br><br>What is the most budget-friendly way to handle these dynamic DNS updates?</p>",
